## Proof

**Setup.** Let $G = \mathbb{Z}/10^{100}\mathbb{Z}$, a cyclic group of order $10^{100} = 2^{100}\cdot 5^{100}$. A cyclic group has a unique subgroup of each order dividing $|G|$. Since $2^n 5^{100-n} \mid 10^{100}$ for every $0 \le n \le 100$, each $A_n$ is the *unique* subgroup of that order.

**CRT decomposition.** By the Chinese Remainder Theorem,
$$G \cong \mathbb{Z}/2^{100}\mathbb{Z} \times \mathbb{Z}/5^{100}\mathbb{Z}.$$
Under this isomorphism, the unique subgroup of order $2^n 5^{100-n}$ factors as
$$A_n \;=\; \underbrace{2^{100-n}\mathbb{Z}/2^{100}\mathbb{Z}}_{\text{order }2^n} \;\times\; \underbrace{5^{n}\mathbb{Z}/5^{100}\mathbb{Z}}_{\text{order }5^{100-n}}.$$

**Membership criterion.** Write $v_2(a)$ for the 2-adic valuation of $a \in \mathbb{Z}/2^{100}$ (with $v_2(0)=100$) and $v_5(b)$ for the 5-adic valuation of $b \in \mathbb{Z}/5^{100}$ (with $v_5(0)=100$). Then
$$a \in 2^{100-n}\mathbb{Z}/2^{100}\mathbb{Z} \iff v_2(a) \ge 100-n \iff n \ge 100 - v_2(a),$$
$$b \in 5^{n}\mathbb{Z}/5^{100}\mathbb{Z} \iff v_5(b) \ge n.$$
So $(a,b) \in A_n$ iff $100 - v_2(a) \le n \le v_5(b)$. Such an $n \in \{0,\dots,100\}$ exists iff
$$v_2(a) + v_5(b) \ge 100.$$

**Counting.** Let $f(i) = \#\{a : v_2(a)=i\}$ and $g(j) = \#\{b : v_5(b)=j\}$:
$$f(i) = \begin{cases} 2^{99-i} & 0 \le i \le 99,\\ 1 & i=100,\end{cases} \qquad g(j) = \begin{cases} 4\cdot 5^{99-j} & 0 \le j \le 99,\\ 1 & j=100.\end{cases}$$

The desired cardinality is
$$S = \sum_{\substack{0\le i,j\le 100\\ i+j\ge 100}} f(i)\,g(j) = \sum_{i=0}^{100} f(i)\!\!\sum_{j=\max(0,\,100-i)}^{100} g(j).$$

For $i=100$: the inner sum is $\sum_{j=0}^{100} g(j) = 5^{100}$, contributing $1\cdot 5^{100} = 5^{100}$.

For $0 \le i \le 99$: the inner sum is
$$\sum_{j=100-i}^{99} 4\cdot 5^{99-j} \;+\; 1 \;=\; \sum_{k=0}^{i-1}4\cdot 5^{k} + 1 \;=\; (5^{i}-1)+1 \;=\; 5^{i},$$
contributing $f(i)\cdot 5^{i} = 2^{99-i}\cdot 5^{i}$.

Therefore
$$S = 5^{100} + \sum_{i=0}^{99} 2^{99-i}\,5^{i} = 5^{100} + 2^{99}\sum_{i=0}^{99}\!\left(\tfrac{5}{2}\right)^{i} = 5^{100} + 2^{99}\cdot\frac{(5/2)^{100}-1}{5/2-1}.$$
$$= 5^{100} + 2^{99}\cdot\frac{2}{3}\!\left(\frac{5^{100}}{2^{100}}-1\right) = 5^{100} + \frac{5^{100}-2^{100}}{3} = \frac{4\cdot 5^{100}-2^{100}}{3}.$$

**Verification (small case).** For $10^{2}=100$: subgroups of orders $25, 10, 4$ are $\langle 4\rangle$, $\langle 10\rangle$, $\langle 25\rangle$. Their union has $25+10-5+2 = 32$ elements, and $\frac{4\cdot 25 - 4}{3} = \frac{96}{3} = 32$. ✓

**Answer.**
$$\boxed{\left|\bigcup_{n=0}^{100} A_n\right| \;=\; \dfrac{4\cdot 5^{100}-2^{100}}{3}}.$$

